--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,271 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any chat member can open **Account settings → Set up chat bots** to create bots.
+The bots page lists all bots owned by that member; administrators can manage all
+bots. Names are required; avatars and webhook URLs are optional. Each bot's code
+is a credential, shown only on its owner's (or an administrator's) bots page.
+
+Bots do **not** join open rooms by default. People still do. Add a bot to chosen
+open or closed rooms using the room's membership controls, or select it when
+starting a direct conversation.
+
+**Add to all open rooms** on the new/edit bot form is off by default. Turning it
+on adds the bot to all existing open rooms and to rooms created or converted to
+open later. Turning it off keeps all current memberships and stops automatic
+joining of future rooms. While it is on, room settings cannot remove the bot
+from an open room. Closed rooms and direct conversations always require an
+explicit invitation. Existing memberships are preserved when upgrading.
+
+## Bots per member
+
+Administrators can set **Bots per member** on **Account settings**. It defaults
+to 5, including on existing accounts, and can be set to 0 to prevent members
+from creating bots. Administrators are exempt.
+
+Each member's non-deleted bots count toward their own limit. Deletion deactivates
+a bot, freeing a place without removing its past messages. Banned and suspended
+bots still count. Lowering the limit never removes bots or prevents editing/deleting them;
+new creation stays blocked until the member is below the current limit.
+
+The setting lives in `Account#settings` using the existing JSON schema, so no
+migration or backfill is needed. `User#can_create_bot?` is shared by the listing,
+new/create controller guard, and create-time model validation. Validation counts
+persisted, non-deactivated bots rather than a cached association or form value.
+Eligibility checks bypass the query cache so model validation cannot reuse a
+stale preflight count or limit from earlier in the request.
+Campfire's SQLite `immediate` save transactions serialize concurrent creations,
+including the count check and insert.
+
+## Posting
+
+
+```sh
+curl --fail-with-body -d "Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅" \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages"
+```
+
+The entire UTF-8 request body is literal message text, not JSON or named form
+fields. HTML is escaped, and line breaks are preserved. For files or multiline
+input, use `--data-binary @filename` or `--data-binary @-` to avoid curl stripping
+line breaks. No browser session or CSRF token is needed.
+
+Responses:
+
+- `201 Created`, with `Location: /messages/:message_id`
+- `422 Unprocessable Content` for empty/whitespace-only or invalid UTF-8 text
+- `404 Not Found` for an unknown room or one the bot cannot access
+- `401 Unauthorized` for an invalid, deleted, or banned bot's code
+- `429 Too Many Requests` during a cooldown, with `Retry-After` in whole seconds
+  remaining (rounded up)
+- `403 Forbidden` for a suspended bot, including the attempt that suspends it
+
+Messages use the ordinary message callbacks, broadcasts, unread tracking, and
+push notification jobs, with the bot as the author.
+
+## Automatic safeguards and reactivation
+
+These limits are fixed in `User::Bot::Safety`, not account settings:
+
+- Each bot can post **10 messages in any rolling 10 seconds**, across all rooms.
+  API posts, mention replies, and joined-room replies all share the same window.
+- The next message is rejected and starts a **30-second cooldown**. API requests
+  return `429` with `Retry-After`; webhook replies are dropped, never deferred.
+  Attempts do not extend the cooldown. **Five additional posting attempts during
+  that same cooldown suspend the bot**. A webhook response that would post a
+  reply counts as an attempt; a callback without a reply does not.
+- **Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries suspend the bot**. Any non-`2xx`
+  status, timeout, or connection error is a failure, including redirects (which
+  are not followed). All `2xx` responses reset the streak, even if there is no
+  usable reply body. Joined and mention deliveries share the streak. Skipped
+  callbacks do not change it; outcomes are recorded as deliveries finish.
+
+Suspension is separate from deletion or banning: it never removes rooms or
+messages, never frees the owner's bot allowance, and never expires on its own.
+Suspended bots stay on the bots page, marked **Suspended**, with a reason. Their
+posting codes return `403` and no new webhook requests are sent. Already queued
+webhooks check status again when run; an in-flight response cannot post a reply
+if the bot has since been suspended.
+
+Only administrators see **Reactivate** on the bots page. It uses Turbo's native
+browser confirmation, and the server also enforces administrator authorization.
+Reactivation keeps the same posting code, rooms, and messages, and clears the
+sliding window, cooldown, attempt count, failure streak, and suspension reason.
+Suspended bots remain in room membership controls so editing a room does not
+silently remove them. Editing a bot (including changing its webhook URL) does
+not reactivate it.
+Repeated reactivation requests for an already active bot do not reset counters.
+
+The guard stores at most ten posting timestamps and its counters on the user
+record. `with_bot_posting` holds a database lock around the check, message insert,
+and window update, so different workers and rooms cannot race past the limit.
+In this application's SQLite configuration, immediate transactions provide the
+write serialization. Rejection exceptions are raised **after** the transaction
+commits, so cooldown/suspension updates survive. Message broadcasts happen after
+the guard returns. Webhook HTTP calls run outside database locks, and their
+outcomes are updated under a short lock. A late success cannot clear suspension.
+Do not replace this with per-process state or a cache that may be disabled.
+Any future bot posting path must use `with_bot_posting` as well.
+
+Deploy with `bin/rails db:migrate` before starting the updated web and job workers.
+
+## Webhooks
+
+### Mentions
+
+Set **Webhook URL** on the new or edit bot form to receive an HTTP POST when a
+person mentions the bot using the room's @-mention autocomplete. Saving an empty
+URL removes the webhook. Bots are suggested alongside people in rooms they can
+access; typing a literal `@name` without selecting a suggestion is not a mention.
+
+The callback has `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages"
+  },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div class=\"action-text-content\">… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`text` removes only this bot's structured mentions and trims surrounding
+whitespace. Other mentions, literal names, formatting-as-text, and line breaks
+remain. `html` contains the full rendered rich-text body, including mentions.
+`reply_to` is this bot's own posting endpoint; `link` opens the room at the
+original message. Both paths are relative to your Campfire origin. Direct rooms
+may have a `null` name.
+
+### Joining a room
+
+A configured webhook also receives a `joined` event when the bot is added to an
+open or closed room, whether automatically or by a person:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+There is no author or message in this event. Direct conversations never send
+`joined`, but still support mentions. A bot receives at most one `joined` per
+room, including if it leaves and rejoins. Repeated room saves, room type changes,
+and toggling automatic membership do not re-send it for existing memberships.
+Adding a webhook later does not announce rooms the bot already belongs to.
+
+### Replying
+
+Both `mention` and `joined` support the same responses. For example, respond to
+`joined` with a greeting and Campfire will post it in that room as the bot.
+
+Respond with:
+
+- `200 OK` and `Content-Type: text/plain`: the UTF-8 response is posted as literal
+  text, preserving line breaks and escaping HTML.
+- `200 OK` and `Content-Type: text/html`: the UTF-8 response is posted as rich text,
+  with the same Action Text sanitization as other messages.
+- `204 No Content`: no reply is posted. You can still reply later by posting text
+  to `reply_to` as described above.
+
+Charset parameters on these content types are accepted. Blank or invalid UTF-8
+bodies, responses larger than 1 MiB, other statuses, and other content types do
+not create a message. Redirects are not followed.
+
+### Delivery and operations
+
+Mention delivery runs in `User::Bot::WebhookJob`, after the original message
+commits. Join delivery runs in `User::Bot::JoinedWebhookJob`, after the membership
+commits. DNS and HTTP never run in the browser request. Production's Resque worker
+must be running. At most one job is queued per bot per message, even when the bot is
+mentioned repeatedly. Edits do not send new callbacks, and bot-authored messages
+do not trigger callbacks (preventing reply loops).
+
+Only active bots with access to the room receive callbacks. Configuration and
+membership are checked again when the job runs, and access is checked before
+posting a synchronous reply. Jobs for deleted records are safely discarded. Network
+failures are logged without URL credentials or payloads and are not automatically
+retried: a timed-out bot may already have processed the request. Bots should use
+`event` plus `message.id` to deduplicate mention deliveries if a job is manually
+replayed. Joined deliveries use a unique bot/room receipt in `bot_room_joins`,
+created in the membership transaction and retained after membership removal.
+The worker atomically claims it before HTTP, preventing duplicate greetings even
+if a job is replayed or two workers run it. As with mentions, failures are not
+retried: this is a single delivery attempt, not guaranteed delivery across network
+failures or a worker crash. Receipts are removed when the bot or room is destroyed.
+
+URLs must use HTTP or HTTPS, without embedded username/password credentials.
+Use HTTPS for private message contents and bot codes. As with link previews,
+Campfire's shared network policy blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses;
+the checked public address is pinned for the connection. Connection, read, and
+write timeouts are each five seconds. For local development, expose the bot
+through a public HTTPS tunnel. The `reply_to` path contains a posting credential:
+keep callback bodies out of public logs and store them securely.
+
+## Implementation and credential safety
+
+Bots are users with the `bot` role and a creator. This reuses avatars and room
+memberships without a separate author type. Bot codes contain 32 random base58
+characters and have a unique database index. Bot management only accepts name,
+avatar, webhook URL, and automatic open-room membership changes, never a role,
+owner, or caller-supplied code.
+
+Deleting a bot deactivates it, removes its shared-room memberships, and immediately
+rejects its code. Its old messages and author identity are retained, just as for
+a deactivated person. Human login and sign-in transfer endpoints exclude bots.
+
+The bots page is served with `Cache-Control: no-store` and is excluded from Turbo's
+page cache. Rails filters bot codes in parameters, model inspection, SQL binds,
+and request paths. The bot endpoint deliberately skips form/JSON parsing so curl's
+default form content type neither changes the message nor logs it as parameters.
+CSRF protection is skipped only on this code-authenticated endpoint.
+
+Treat bot codes like passwords: keep them out of source control and CI output,
+store them in CI secrets, and use HTTPS. Database backups contain bot credentials
+and need the same protection as other account data. Configure reverse proxies,
+access logs, and external tracing to redact `/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages`
+URLs or omit these requests; those systems see the URL before Rails can filter it.
+If a code is exposed, delete the bot and create a replacement.
+
+## Safeguard regression tests
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots/message_safety_test.rb test/controllers/bots/reactivations_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/jobs/user/bot/webhook_safety_test.rb test/models/user/bot_safety_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bot_safety_test.rb
+```
+
+Coverage includes rolling-window boundaries, cooldown expiry and rounding,
+concurrent posts, independent bots, combined API/webhook posting, both callback
+events, failed deliveries and all `2xx` success resets, in-flight suspension,
+preserved data and bot-limit usage, and administrator-only reactivation. The
+system tests exercise both cancelling and accepting the native confirmation.
+
+## Regression tests
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/user/bot_rooms_test.rb test/jobs/user/bot/joined_webhook_job_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb test/controllers/rooms/opens_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb
+```
+
+People retain the bulk membership-insert path. Bot grants use normal membership
+creation so the callback also covers direct association creation. The unique
+membership index makes repeated grants idempotent; the unique join receipt index
+keeps deduplication independent of the lifetime of a membership. Keep these paths
+in mind if adding new room-grant mechanisms: raw membership inserts bypass bot
+callbacks.
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_request.rb b/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_request.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..59bf718
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_request.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# Bot messages are raw text even when curl uses its default form content type.
+# Do not parse (or log) their bodies as form fields or JSON. Codes in URL path
+# segments also need explicit redaction: Rails normally only filters the query.
+module BotMessageRequest
+  PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages/?)(?=\?|\z)}
+
+  def request_parameters
+    if post? && path.match?(PATH)
+      {}
+    else
+      super
+    end
+  end
+
+  def filtered_path
+    super.sub(PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+  end
+
+  def filtered_env
+    super.transform_values do |value|
+      value.is_a?(String) ? value.sub(PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2') : value
+    end
+  end
+end
+
+ActionDispatch::Request.prepend BotMessageRequest
